      en: "The Sangtuda 1 Hydroelectric Power Plant on the Vakhsh River is a strategic energy project and a cornerstone of economic cooperation between Tajikistan and Russia. Construction originally began in the 1980s but was halted due to the civil war, only resuming in 2005 with significant Russian backing. With an installed capacity of 670 megawatts, it ranks as one of the largest power plants in the country. It operates four turbines and supplies approximately 12 percent of Tajikistan's total electricity demand. The facility is managed by a joint venture in which the Russian company Inter RAO holds a majority stake. Beyond domestic supply, Sangtuda 1 is instrumental in exporting electricity to neighboring countries, most notably Afghanistan. The plant is vital for mitigating seasonal energy shortages during the harsh winter months in Tajikistan.",
